Basic OSPF Configuration
R2
router ospf 1
 router-id 2.2.2.2
 network 10.23.23.0 0.0.0.3 area 0
R3
router ospf 1
 router-id 3.3.3.3
 network 10.23.23.0 0.0.0.3 area 0

The 10.23.23.0/30 network allows R2 and R3 to form their OSPF neighbor adjacency in Area 0.

OSPF Loopbacks

Loopback interfaces provide stable router IDs and additional networks that can be advertised through OSPF.

R2
interface loopback0
 ip address 2.2.2.2 255.255.255.255

interface loopback1
 ip address 22.22.22.2 255.255.255.255
R3
interface loopback0
 ip address 3.3.3.3 255.255.255.255

interface loopback1
 ip address 33.33.33.3 255.255.255.255
Passive Interfaces

VLAN subinterfaces and loopback interfaces can be made passive when their networks should be advertised through OSPF but they should not form OSPF neighbor adjacencies.

Example:

router ospf 1
 passive-interface loopback0
 passive-interface loopback1

The passive interface continues to advertise its connected network into OSPF, but OSPF Hello packets are not sent through that interface.

OSPF VLAN Networks

R2 and R3 use Router-on-a-Stick subinterfaces for the VLAN gateways.

The VLAN networks are:

192.168.10.0/24
192.168.20.0/24
192.168.30.0/24
192.168.40.0/24
192.168.50.0/24
192.168.60.0/24

These networks can be advertised into OSPF while the VLAN subinterfaces remain passive.

Example:

router ospf 1
 network 192.168.10.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
 network 192.168.20.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
 network 192.168.30.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
 network 192.168.40.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
 network 192.168.50.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
 network 192.168.60.0 0.0.0.255 area 0

The VLAN interfaces can then be configured as passive interfaces because they are used to advertise networks rather than form OSPF neighbor relationships.

OSPF Authentication

OSPF authentication can be configured on the R2-R3 Area 0 adjacency.

Example:

interface GigabitEthernet0/1
 ip ospf authentication message-digest
 ip ospf message-digest-key 1 md5 CCNP

The authentication configuration must match on both sides of the OSPF adjacency.

OSPF Area Summarization

R3 operates as the ABR between Area 0 and Area 10.

The Area 10 loopback network used in the lab is:

33.33.33.3/32

The corresponding Area 10 summary configuration is:

router ospf 1
 area 10 range 33.33.33.3 255.255.255.255

This represents the specific /32 prefix associated with the Area 10 network.

For a larger production-style Area 10 design containing multiple contiguous networks, a larger summary prefix could be used.

Default Route

R2 is configured with a static default route pointing toward R3.

Next hop:

10.23.23.2

Configuration:

ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 10.23.23.2

The default route can then be injected into OSPF:

router ospf 1
 default-information originate

This allows R2 to advertise the default route into the OSPF domain.

OSPF Stub Area

A standard stub area blocks external Type 5 LSAs and uses a default route instead.

Configuration:

router ospf 1
 area 10 stub
Totally Stubby Area

A totally stubby area blocks external LSAs and inter-area summary LSAs, relying on a default route from the ABR.

Configuration:

router ospf 1
 area 10 stub no-summary
OSPF NSSA

A Not-So-Stubby Area (NSSA) allows external routes to be introduced into the area while restricting external LSAs entering from outside.

Configuration:

router ospf 1
 area 10 nssa
NSSA Totally Stubby Area

An NSSA can also be configured to restrict inter-area routes while still allowing controlled external route injection.

Configuration:

router ospf 1
 area 10 nssa no-summary
OSPF LSA Types

The lab demonstrates the major OSPF LSA concepts:

Type 1 — Router LSA

Generated by every OSPF router and describes the router's links within an area.

Type 2 — Network LSA

Generated by the DR on multi-access networks and describes the routers connected to the network segment.

Type 3 — Summary LSA

Generated by an ABR to advertise networks from one OSPF area into another.

Type 4 — ASBR Summary LSA

Generated by an ABR to provide reachability information toward an ASBR.

Type 5 — External LSA

Generated by an ASBR when external routes are redistributed into OSPF.

Type 7 — NSSA External LSA

Generated inside an NSSA when external routes are introduced into the area.

